=================================
# QUESTION : SCALING KYA HAI ?
=================================

Scaling is the process of increasing the system's capacity to handle the increased workload.

---------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : SCALING KI ZARURAT KYA HAI ?
---------------------------------------------

Sabse pehle ek simple E-commerce Spring Boot Application lete hain.

Spring Boot application ke andar ek embedded application server (e.g. Tomcat) hota hai,
aur ye application server ek Physical/Virtual server par run karta hai.Yaani Application Server 
ek software hai, jo hamesha kisi Physical Server ya Virtual Server ke upar hi run karta hai.


Architecture:

             Users
                |
                |
                v
        ----------------
        | Spring Boot  |
        | Application  |
        ----------------
                |
                |
                v
             Database


Ab is Physical/Virtual server and application server (Tomcat) dono ke paas apne limited resources hote hain.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : SERVER RESOURCES AUR APPLICATION RESOURCES ME FARAK KYA HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

### SERVER RESOURCES
----------------------

CPU              : 2 Cores
RAM              : 4 GB
Disk             : 100 GB
Network Card     : 1 Gbps



### APPLICATION RESOURCES
--------------------------

1: WORKER THREADS :
--------------------

Har incoming HTTP request ko process karne ke liye Tomcat ek worker thread allocate karta hai.

Suppose:

Maximum Threads = 200

Matlab Tomcat ek time par maximum 200 requests ko actively process kar sakta hai (assuming each request occupies one worker thread).

Isse zyada requests aane par:

- Accept Queue me wait karti hain, ya
- Overload hone par reject ho jaati hain

(OS / Tomcat configuration par depend karta hai.)



2: DATABASE CONNECTIONS :
-------------------------

Spring Boot application database se communicate karne ke liye connection pool use karti hai.

Suppose:

Maximum Pool Size = 100

Matlab ek time par application maximum 100 active database connections use kar sakti hai.



-----------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : TRAFFIC BADHNE PAR SYSTEM PAR KYA ASAR PADTA HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------------------

## INITIALLY EVERYTHING WORKS FINE :
------------------------------------

Suppose application ke sirf 100 users hain, traffic kam hai, server ke resources sufficient hain. Application smoothly chal rahi hai.

Users : 100
Traffic : 10 Requests / Second
Status : Sab smooth


## COMPANY STARTS GROWING :
---------------------------
Kuch months baad users 10,000 ho gaye aur traffic 500 RPS ho gayi — server ab bhi chal raha tha, lekin resources gradually saturate hone lage.

Aur kuch months baad users 100,000 aur traffic 2,000 RPS ho gayi — ab actual problems start ho gayi.



## QUESTION: KYA EXACTLY FAIL HOTA HAI ?
------------------------------------------

1. TOMCAT THREADS GET EXHAUSTED
---------------------------------

Suppose Tomcat Worker Threads = 200, aur ek request ko complete hone me 2 seconds lag rahe hain.

Agar ek hi time par 500 requests aa jaati hain:

Pehli 200 Requests → worker thread mil jayega
Baaki 300 Requests → immediately process nahi ho paayengi

Wo:

Accept Queue me wait karengi (agar queue me jagah hai)

Ya queue full hone par reject ho sakti hain

Result:

High Response Time → Timeouts → 5xx Errors



2. CPU BECOMES A BOTTLENECK
------------------------------

Har HTTP request ko process karne ke liye ek worker thread allocate hota hai,
aur us thread ka code CPU par execute hota hai.


## CPU CORES vs THREADS
--------------------------

Lekin CPU ke paas limited cores hote hain (jaise 2, 4, 8 cores),
jabki threads ki sankhya usse kaafi zyada ho sakti hai (jaise 200 threads).

Suppose:

CPU Cores = 4
Tomcat Worker Threads = 200

CPU ek time par sirf 4 threads ko hi parallel execute kar sakta hai (assuming 1 thread per core at a time).

Baaki 196 threads runnable state me wait karte hain. Unhe CPU time milne ka intezaar hota hai.

OS CPU time ko threads ke beech share karta hai, aur is process ko Context Switching kehte hain.

Context switching me OS pehle ek running thread ki current state save karta hai, phir doosre thread ki saved state load karta hai,
aur us thread ko thoda CPU time deta hai. Ye process OS baaki threads ke saath bhi repeat karta rehta hai.

Har context switch kuch CPU time consume karta hai, jise context switching overhead kehte hain.



## RPS vs CONCURRENT REQUESTS
------------------------------

Dhyan rakhne wali baat: RPS aur Concurrent Requests alag cheezein hain.

    RPS                 = Ek second me kitni requests aati hain (throughput)
    Concurrent Requests = Ek time par kitni requests actively process ho rahi hain

    Max RPS = Number of Threads / Average Request Processing Time (sec)


### EXAMPLE
-----------

    Tomcat Worker Threads            = 200
    Average Request Processing Time  = 100 ms (0.1 sec)

    Max RPS = 200 / 0.1 = 2000 RPS

Matlab 200 threads ke saath bhi 2000 RPS handle ho sakti hai,
kyunki har thread 1 second me 10 requests process kar leta hai.



## LOW TRAFFIC vs HIGH TRAFFIC
--------------------------------

Jab traffic kam hoti hai, i.e. kam requests application par aati hain:

    10 RPS  → Kam threads simultaneously active hote hain
            → CPU par kam context switching hoti hai
            → Context switching ka overhead kam rehta hai
              (i.e. CPU time context switching me waste nahi hota)
            → CPU ka zyadatar time actual application logic execute karne me lagta hai
            → CPU utilization = 15%

Jab traffic badhta hai, i.e. zyada requests application par aati hain:

    500 RPS → Zyada threads simultaneously active ho jaate hain
            → Context switching badh jaati hai
            → CPU ka kuch time context switching overhead me chala jaata hai
            → CPU utilization = 75%

Jab traffic bahut zyada hoti hai:

    2000 RPS → Almost saare 200 threads continuously runnable ho jaate hain
             → CPU continuously context switching karta rehta hai
             → CPU utilization = 100%
             → Actual application logic execute karne ka time kam milta hai
             → Har thread ko CPU time kam milta hai
             → Response time increase
             → Latency badhne lagti hai




4. RAM BECOMES A BOTTLENECK
--------------------------------

Sabse pehle ye samajhna zaroori hai ki physical/virtual server jispe
Spring Boot application deployed hai, uski total RAM us JVM 
process ko nahi milti jisme Spring Boot application (embedded Tomcat ke saath)
run karti hai.

Suppose server ki Total RAM = 4 GB.

Ye 4 GB kai cheezon me divide hoti hai:

    - Operating System (OS)         → kuch memory
    - Other processes/services      → kuch memory
    - JVM (Tomcat)                  → kuch memory

Yaani JVM ko sirf ek portion milta hai — poori 4 GB nahi.

Aur JVM ke andar bhi ye space further divide hoti hai:

    JVM
    ├── Heap                 
    │
    ├── Stack  
    │
    ├── Metaspace → Class metadata, method metadata
    │
    └── Other areas → Code cache, JIT compiled code, etc.


## HEAP vs STACK
-----------------

HEAP:
    - Shared area across all threads.
    - Stores actual objects (Request/Response objects,
      Hibernate entities, Session data, etc.).

STACK:
    - Private per thread. Har thread ka apna alag stack hota hai.
    - Jab bhi koi thread method call karta hai, us method call
      ke liye stack par ek STACK FRAME push hota hai.


Ek Stack Frame me store hota hai:

    - Method ke parameters
    - Method ke local primitive variables (int, boolean, char, etc.)
    - Method ke local object references (heap me stored object ka address)
    - Return address



## TRAFFIC BADHNE PAR KYA HOTA HAI?
-------------------------------------

Initially:

    Heap Usage = 1 GB / 4 GB

Traffic badha:

    Heap Usage = 3.5 GB / 4 GB

Traffic aur badha:

    Heap Usage = 4 GB / 4 GB


Ab JVM heap almost full ho jaati hai.

Is situation me JVM ko Garbage Collection (GC) frequently run karni
padti hai, taaki unused objects ko reclaim karke memory free ki ja sake.

Problem:

GC jab memory reclaim karti hai, to kuch phases me application
threads ko temporarily stop karna pad sakta hai
(Stop-The-World pauses).

Isse:

    - Response time badh jaata hai (i.e. Latency increase)

Aur agar memory iske baad bhi insufficient rehti hai, to application
OutOfMemoryError throw kar sakti hai.


## IMPORTANT:
---------------

Method call hone par stack frame push hota hai,
method return hone par frame automatically pop ho jaata hai.
Isliye stack memory automatically free hoti hai —
koi manual cleanup ki zaroorat nahi padti.

Note: GC sirf Heap ko clean karta hai, Stack ko nahi.




5. NETWORK BANDWIDTH ALSO HAS A LIMIT
----------------------------------------

Suppose server ka Network Interface Card (NIC) support karta hai:

    1 Gbps

Lekin incoming + outgoing traffic ho gaya:

    3 Gbps

Ye possible nahi hai.

Network packets queue hone lagenge, kyunki NIC apni capacity se
zyada data transmit nahi kar sakta.

Result:

    Latency increase
    Throughput degrade



## FINAL OBSERVATION
---------------------

Dhyan se dekho — application me koi bug nahi aaya.

Problem sirf itni hai ki:

    Existing Workload  >  Server Capacity

ho gayi hai.


## WHAT IS THE ROOT CAUSE?
----------------------------

Ek single server / application ke resources finite (limited) hote hain.

In sab ki ek upper limit hoti hai.

Jaise-jaise traffic increase hota hai, ye resources gradually
saturate hone lagte hain.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION : TOMCAT THREAD POOL KO HAMESHA KYUN NAHI BADHANA CHAHIYE ZYADA REQUESTS HANDLE KARNE KE LIYE? ?
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    Tomcat Worker Threads = 200

Aur ek hi time par 500 requests aa rahi hain.

Sawaal:

Kya hum simply thread pool ko 200 → 1000 kar dein?
Isse to ek time par zyada requests process ho jayengi.

Answer:

Generally, NO.

Iske peeche multiple reasons hain.


1. MORE THREADS REQUIRE MORE STACK MEMORY
------------------------------------------

Har thread create hone par JVM uske liye ek dedicated private Thread Stack
allocate karta hai. Ye stack method execution frames, us method ke local
primitive variables, aur us method ke andar create hue objects ke object
references store karta hai.

Har thread ka apna alag stack hota hai, jo doosre threads ke saath share
nahi hota.

Suppose:

    Per Thread Stack Size = 1 MB

Agar 1000 threads create kiye, to approximately 1 GB space sirf thread
stacks ke liye reserve ho jaati hai.

Lekin dhyan rakhne wali baat:

    - Physical/virtual server ki TOTAL RAM poori ki poori JVM ko nahi
      milti. OS aur doosre processes bhi apna portion use karte hain.

    - JVM ko jo portion milta hai, uske andar bhi kai areas hote hain:
          Heap, Stack Area, Metaspace, Code Cache, etc.

    - Ye saare areas apni-apni configured limits ke saath coexist karte
      hain. Isliye Stack Area ke liye available space actually limited
      hoti hai.

Agar bahut zyada threads create kiye jaayein, to unke thread stacks
kaafi portion consume karne lagte hain, jisse overall memory pressure
create ho sakta hai.



2. MORE THREADS DO NOT MEAN MORE PARALLEL EXECUTION (CONTEXT SWITCHING OVERHEAD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    CPU Cores              = 4
    Tomcat Worker Threads  = 1000

Dekho, CPU ke paas sirf 4 cores hain, lekin threads 1000 hain.

Ek 4-core CPU ek time par sirf 4 threads ko hi actually execute kar sakta hai
(assuming ek core par ek thread). Baaki 996 threads wait karte rehte hain.

Ab OS CPU time ko in sabhi threads ke beech share karta hai. Iske liye
CPU ko baar-baar ek thread ki state save karni padti hai aur doosre thread
ki state load karni padti hai. Is process ko Context Switching kehte hain.

Aur har context switch CPU time consume karta hai.

To jab threads ki sankhya CPU cores se bahut zyada ho jaati hai, tab CPU
apna zyadatar time context switching me hi laga deta hai — actual
application logic execute karne me nahi.

Result:

    - CPU overhead badh jaata hai
    - Throughput degrade ho jaata hai
    - Response Time aur bhi badh sakta hai



3. THREAD POOL BADHANE PAR BHI DATABASE BOTTLENECK BAN SAKTA HAI
-----------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    Tomcat Threads   = 1000
    DB Connections   = 100

Ab 1000 requests database access karna chahti hain.

Sirf 100 requests ko DB connection milega.

Baaki 900 requests connection available hone ka wait karengi.

Isliye thread pool increase karne ke baad bhi database bottleneck
hi rahega.



4. DOWNSTREAM SERVICES BHI BOTTLENECK BAN SAKTI HAIN
-------------------------------------------------------

Suppose application Payment Service ko HTTP call karti hai.

Agar 1000 threads ek saath Payment Service ko requests bhejne lagein,
to Payment Service khud overload ho sakti hai.

Result:

    Timeouts
    Connection Failures
    Cascading Failures

Yaani problem sirf tumhari application tak limited nahi rahegi —
poora distributed system impact ho sakta hai.



